    "text": "# Exact Rational Polynomial Interpolation\n\n## 1. Mathematical Background\n\n### 1.1 Problem Statement\n\nGiven $n+1$ distinct points $(x_0, y_0), (x_1, y_1), \\ldots, (x_n, y_n)$ with $x_i \\neq x_j$ for $i \\neq j$, we seek a **rational function** $R(x) = \\frac{P(x)}{Q(x)}$ where:\n- $P(x)$ is a polynomial of degree at most $m$\n- $Q(x)$ is a polynomial of degree at most $k$\n- $Q(x_i) \\neq 0$ for all $i$\n- $R(x_i) = y_i$ for all $i$\n\nThe total number of free parameters is $(m+1) + (k+1) = m + k + 2$. To have a unique solution (up to scaling), we need $m + k + 1 = n$, i.e., $m + k = n - 1$.\n\n### 1.2 The Linear System\n\nThe interpolation conditions $R(x_i) = y_i$ give:\n",
